      <title>Xi Jinping’s corruption crackdown in the spotlight as China prepares for meeting of top leaders</title>
      <link>https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/xi-jinping-corruption-crackdown-china-fifth-plenum</link>
      <description>&lt;p&gt;The fifth plenum comes against a backdrop of years of purges that have had some analysts wondering if the issue is corruption or a lack of loyalty&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;When Xi Jinping came to power in 2012, he pledged to stamp out corruption all through the ranks of the Communist party. More than a decade later, the pace of investigations has not slowed. Last year, China’s disciplinary authorities opened investigations into more than 1 million people, including more than 100 officials at or above ministerial or provincial level.&lt;/p&gt;&lt;p&gt;As Chinese Communist party (CCP) officials kick off an important meeting this week, discipline – and party loyalty &lt;strong&gt;–&lt;/strong&gt; is high on the agenda. For those who try to read the tea leaves of elite Chinese politics, concern about the apparent lack of discipline is dominating CCP discussions.&lt;/p&gt; &lt;a href="https://www.theguardian.com/world/2026/oct/26/xi-jinping-corruption-crackdown-china-fifth-plenum"&gt;Continue reading...&lt;/a&gt;</description>
